---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc235.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Significant Accounting Policies Note" },
        { id: "s4", label: 'What Counts as a "Significant" Policy' },
        { id: "s5", label: "Location & Order of Notes" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — Drafting a Policy Disclosure" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s9", label: "ASC 235 vs IAS 1's Accounting Policy Disclosures" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Understanding the numbers requires understanding the choices behind them</div><div class="principle">Two entities with identical underlying economics can report different figures purely because of different, equally acceptable accounting policy choices (e.g. FIFO vs weighted-average inventory costing). ASC 235 requires disclosure of the SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES an entity has selected, so users can properly interpret and compare the financial statements — a disclosure requirement broadly analogous to the accounting policy disclosure requirements found in IAS 1, though housed as its own dedicated Topic in the Codification.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Applies to all entities</div><div class="principle">ASC 235 applies to all entities preparing US GAAP financial statements — the requirement to disclose significant accounting policies is essentially universal, regardless of entity size or public/private status.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Significant Accounting Policies Note</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A dedicated, typically first, note</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Financial statements should include a disclosure of all SIGNIFICANT accounting policies as an INTEGRAL PART of the statements — in practice, almost universally presented as the FIRST note (commonly titled "Summary of Significant Accounting Policies"), covering the specific principles and methods used in preparing the statements, including the criteria and methods applied to select from among ACCEPTABLE ALTERNATIVES, and to apply GAAP to the entity's own particular circumstances.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>What Counts as a "Significant" Policy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Judgement-driven, not a mandated checklist</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Disclosure should be given particular prominence for policies involving a selection from among ACCEPTABLE ALTERNATIVES, policies UNIQUE to the entity's specific industry, and policies involving UNUSUALLY significant application of GAAP to the entity's particular facts. Routine, non-controversial policies with no meaningful choice involved warrant less prominent (or no) disclosure — a genuinely judgement-driven threshold rather than a fixed checklist, echoing the same materiality-based philosophy the IFRS Practice Statement on Making Materiality Judgements applies to IFRS accounting policy disclosures.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Location &amp; Order of Notes</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Convention, not a strict codified sequence</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">While ASC 235 does not mandate an exact ordering of subsequent notes, US practice conventionally places the Significant Accounting Policies note FIRST, followed by notes elaborating on specific balance sheet and income statement line items, then broader disclosures (commitments, contingencies, subsequent events, segment information) — a convention broadly similar to how IFRS preparers typically structure their own notes, though neither framework STRICTLY mandates a specific note sequence.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Drafting a Policy Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Choosing what warrants prominent disclosure</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Inventory costing method disclosure</div><div class="wk-body">A manufacturing entity uses the FIFO method for costing inventory — one of several acceptable methods under US GAAP (FIFO, weighted-average, or LIFO for entities that elect it).</div><div class="wk-answer">Because inventory costing involves a genuine SELECTION FROM ACCEPTABLE ALTERNATIVES with a material effect on reported cost of goods sold and inventory value, this policy WARRANTS prominent disclosure in the Significant Accounting Policies note — e.g. "Inventory is stated at the lower of cost, determined using the first-in, first-out (FIFO) method, or net realisable value." A routine matter with no real choice (e.g. that cash includes demand deposits) would not need the same prominence.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 235 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>A description of all significant accounting policies, presented as an integral part of the financial statements</span><span>Particular prominence for policies involving a selection among acceptable alternatives, industry-specific policies, and unusual applications of GAAP</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The central judgement under ASC 235 is determining WHICH policies are significant enough to warrant disclosure — too little detail leaves users unable to understand the basis for reported figures, while excessive boilerplate detail on routine, non-judgemental policies dilutes the note's usefulness, echoing the "disclosure overload" concern that motivated the IFRS materiality guidance discussed elsewhere in this library.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>ASC 235 vs IAS 1's Accounting Policy Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 235 vs IAS 1</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 235)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 1)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Dedicated Topic</span><span>Yes — its own standalone Topic in the Codification</span><span>No separate standard; accounting policy disclosure requirements sit within IAS 1 itself</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Materiality-based threshold</span><span>"Significant" policies, with prominence for genuine alternatives/judgement</span><span>2021 IAS 1 amendments similarly shifted emphasis toward MATERIAL, entity-specific policies over generic boilerplate — a closely converged philosophy</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Typical placement</span><span>Conventionally the first note</span><span>Conventionally the first or second note, similarly by market convention rather than strict mandate</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">This is one of the more philosophically converged disclosure areas — both frameworks have moved, over time, toward emphasising genuinely decision-useful, entity-specific policy disclosure over routine boilerplate.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where ASC 235's requirement lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Significant accounting policies disclosure</span><span>Notes only</span><span>No direct recognition/measurement impact on any primary statement</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 205</span><span>Supplies the overall complete-set-of-financial-statements framework this Topic's notes accompany</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 250</span><span>Governs disclosure of CHANGES in accounting principles, complementary to this Topic's disclosure of the policies themselves</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Accepted alternatives</div><div class="glossary-def">Situations where more than one acceptable accounting method exists under GAAP for the same type of transaction — the primary trigger for prominent policy disclosure under ASC 235.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Integral part of the financial statements</div><div class="glossary-def">The characterisation ASC 235 gives to the accounting policies note — it is not optional supplementary material, but a required component of a complete set of financial statements.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Summary of Significant Accounting Policies</div><div class="glossary-def">The conventional title for the note required by ASC 235, almost universally presented as the first note to the financial statements.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Industry-specific policy</div><div class="glossary-def">An accounting policy unique to the entity's particular industry — one of the categories ASC 235 flags for particularly prominent disclosure.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Accepted alternatives", definition: "Situations where more than one acceptable accounting method exists under GAAP for the same type of transaction — the primary trigger for prominent policy disclosure under ASC 235." },
      { term: "Integral part of the financial statements", definition: "The characterisation ASC 235 gives to the accounting policies note — it is not optional supplementary material, but a required component of a complete set of financial statements." },
      { term: "Summary of Significant Accounting Policies", definition: "The conventional title for the note required by ASC 235, almost universally presented as the first note to the financial statements." },
      { term: "Industry-specific policy", definition: "An accounting policy unique to the entity's particular industry — one of the categories ASC 235 flags for particularly prominent disclosure." }
    ]} />

  <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC235} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
